## Three findings that change how this gets built

**1. DIAN's CUNE worked example does not reproduce.** I ran a 1,344-variant search — 7 hour formats, 4 document-number formats, padded PIN/`Ambiente`, parenthesised and newline-terminated strings, three encodings, five hash algorithms. Nothing matches the published value. The formula is sound; the example is wrong (the annex even cites `sha1-online.com` as its reference tool for a SHA-384 value). It's the natural unit-test fixture, so this would have burned a day or more.

**2. No signed reference instance exists.** The toolkit's "Ejemplificaciones" XMLs are skeletons — every string `"A"`, every date `9999-12-31`, `<ext:UBLExtensions>` empty with no signature. So the XAdES profile has no offline oracle either. Two of three cryptographic controls can only be proven in habilitación, which is why P6 is scoped as a convergence loop rather than a certification gate.

**3. The auth model is documented two incompatible ways.** The Anexo says mutual TLS; the WSDL policy says `RequireClientCertificate="false"` with the X.509 as a message-level endorsing token, thumbprint-referenced. I've resolved it to the WSDL and given the corroboration, but flagged it for empirical confirmation — same certificate serves either, so testing both is cheap.

Also worth knowing: **rule 90 makes transmission non-idempotent** (one document number per worker, ever), which drives the retry, concurrency and correction design more than anything else.

